---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
import scans from "../data/scans.json";
---

<Doc
  title="About — mcpwatchman"
  description="mcpwatchman is an independent, free audit of the servers in the official Model Context Protocol registry: a safety layer over the registry, not a competitor to it."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/about/"
  heading="About mcpwatchman"
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      An independent, continuous security and quality audit of the servers
      listed in the official Model Context Protocol registry. Every server we
      publish gets a page: a per-axis score with the evidence behind every
      deduction, or a plain statement of what could not be assessed and why.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="registry-heading">
    <h2 id="registry-heading">A layer over the registry, not a rival to it</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io">official MCP registry</a>{" "}
      lists what servers exist and where to get them. It holds metadata by
      design and makes no claim about whether a server is safe to run.
      mcpwatchman reads that list and adds the missing layer: what the
      published code, dependencies and repository actually show.
    </p>
    <p>
      We keep no listings of our own. A server is here only if the registry
      lists it, and we scan the version the registry names wherever that
      version can be found in the source. When the registry
      deprecates or drops a server, its page says so and keeps the last scan
      taken while it was listed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="independent-heading">
    <h2 id="independent-heading">Independent, and free</h2>
    <p>
      mcpwatchman is not affiliated with Anthropic, with the Model Context
      Protocol project or with the registry. Nobody pays for a score, and
      there is nothing a maintainer can buy to change one; a finding changes
      when the code does, or when we are shown it was wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site, the data, the API, the feed and the CLI are free, and the
      methodology, rules and scoring engine are{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/kVadrum/mcpwatchman">public</a>, so any score
      can be reproduced and argued with. It is run by{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/kVadrum">kVadrum</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
    <h2 id="how-heading">How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Each night the published servers are rescanned: source, manifests and
      lockfiles are read, never executed, and no running server is contacted.
      Five axes are scored independently — see{" "}
      <a href="/methodology/">the methodology</a>. {scans.length} servers are
      published today. The set is pinned and only grows: a page, once
      published, keeps its URL. It is not a random sample of the registry, so
      a server's absence is never a judgement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="not-heading">
    <h2 id="not-heading">What it is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Not a registry.</strong> We score the servers the official registry lists.</li>
      <li><strong>Not runtime monitoring.</strong> The analysis is static; behaviour at runtime is outside it.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a verdict.</strong> A score summarises measurable signals at a point in time. Whether to install a server is the reader's call.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="licence-heading">
    <h2 id="licence-heading">Licence</h2>
